                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in P(x, y)
          %h3#unordered_pairing Unordered pairing
          %p
            The
            %strong axiom of unordered pairing
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong axiom of unordered pairs
          %p
            %strong> Axiom of unordered pairing
            \: If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are (material) sets, then there exists a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in P
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in P
            and for all sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in P
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = x
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = y
          %p
            Using the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+extensionality"} axiom of extensionality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is unique; it is usually denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x,y\}
            and called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair+set"} pair set
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x,x\}
            may also be denoted simply
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x\}
          %p
            One could also assume that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            has a primitive binary operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
            which takes of a material set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            and returns a material set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x, y\}
          %p
            %strong> Axiom of unordered pairing
            \: If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are (material) sets, then
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \{x, y\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in \{x, y\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in \{x, y\}
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = x
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = y
          %h3#ordered_pairing Ordered pairing
          %p
            Let us assume that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            has a primitive binary operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-,-)
            which takes of a material set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            and returns a material set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y)
          %p
            The
            %strong axiom of ordered pairing
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong axiom of ordered pairs
          %p
            %strong> Axiom of ordered pairing
            \:
            %em
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              are (material) sets, then
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in (x, y)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in (x, y)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, b) = (x, y)
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = x
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b = y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi a
                  %mo .
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi b
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall a.\forall b.\{a, b\} = \{x, y\} \iff (a = x \wedge b = y)
          %h3#with_sets_and_elements_different With sets and elements different
          %p
            In set theories where sets and elements are not the same thing, pairing becomes an operation on both the sets and the elements. One has to add a primitive ternary relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(X, Y, P)
            which says that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X, P, c, a)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2(Y, P, c, b)
            which says that element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in X
            is the left element of the pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in P
            and element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in Y
            is the right element of the pair
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in P
          %p
            %strong> Axiom of ordered pairing
            \:
            %em
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              are sets, then there exists a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(X, Y, P)
              and for every object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in Y
              implies that there exists an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in P
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X, P, c, a)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2(Y, P, c, b)
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is usually denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times Y
            and called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is usually denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, b)
            and called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+pair"} ordered pair
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
          %h3#in_dependent_type_theory In dependent type theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarski+universe"} Tarski universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \in)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+sets"} pure sets
            which behaves as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊢
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo :
                        %mi V
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:V \vdash \sum_{y:V} y \in x
            %strong axiom of pairing
            is given by the following
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rule"} inference rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} pairing
                        %mi V
                      %mo :
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi V
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi y
                          %mo :
                          %mi V
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                          %mo :
                          %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}}{\Gamma \vdash \mathrm{pairing}_V:\prod_{x:V} \prod_{y:V} \sum_{P:V} (x \in P) \times (y \in P)}
          %h2#generalisation Generalisation
          %p
            The axiom of pairing is the binary part of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary%2Fnullary+pair"} binary/nullary pair
            whose nullary part is the axiom stating the existence of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+union"} axiom of union
            to prove every instance of the following
            %strong axiom
            (or rather theorem)
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong schema of finite sets
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, \ldots, x_n
              are sets, then there exists a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, \ldots, x_n \in P
          %p
            Again, we can prove the existence of specific
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, \ldots, x_n
            are the
            %em only
            members of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and prove that this
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is unique; it is denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_1, \ldots, x_n\}
            and is called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            consisting of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, \ldots, x_n
          %p
            Note that this is a
            = succeed "," do
              %em schema
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
            over the (internal) set of natural numbers. In particular, each instance of this schema can be stated and proved without the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} axiom of infinity
          %ul
            %li
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
            %li
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \coloneqq x_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \coloneqq x_1
              to construct
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_1\}
            %li
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \coloneqq x_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \coloneqq x_2
              to construct
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_1, x_2\}
            %li
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_1, x_2\}
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_3\}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big\{\{x_1, x_2\}, \{x_3\}\big\}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_1, x_2, x_3\}
            %li
              In general, once we have
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_1, \ldots, x_{n-1}\}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_n\}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big\{\{x_1, \ldots, x_{n-1}\}, \{x_n\}\big\}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_1, \ldots, x_n\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \gt 3
              can actually go faster than this; the length of the shortest proof is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logarithmic"} logarithmic
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              rather than linear in
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Note that these ‘finite sets’ are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kuratowski-finite+sets"} Kuratowski-finite sets
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            treatment.
          %h2#related_notions Related notions
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pairing+structure"} pairing structure
          %p
            In the
            = succeed "Lab," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pairing"} pairing
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+pair"} ordered
            pairs.
          %h2#references References
          %p For the axiom of ordered pairing see:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%A5kon+Robbestad+Gylterud"}> Håkon Robbestad Gylterud
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elisabeth+Stenholm"} Elisabeth Stenholm
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niccol%C3%B2+Veltri"} Niccolò Veltri
              %em Terminal Coalgebras and Non-wellfounded Sets in Homotopy Type Theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.06696"}> arXiv:2001.06696
              \&amp;rbrack;
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/foundational+axiom"} foundational axiom
